userStories.description:
Backend routes for creating, listing, and deleting git worktrees in any registered workspace. Uses the simple-git package already installed in Phase 1.

File: ~/rorcha/backend/src/routes/worktrees.ts

```typescript
import simpleGit from 'simple-git';
import { db } from '../db';
import { broadcast } from '../lib/sse-hub';
```

New endpoints:

GET /api/worktrees?workspaceId=
- Runs `git worktree list --porcelain` in the workspace path
- Parses output into array of { path, branch, headSha, isLocked, isBare }
- Returns { worktrees: GitWorktree[] }

```typescript
interface GitWorktree {
  path: string;
  branch: string;
  headSha: string;
  isLocked: boolean;
  isBare: boolean;
  addedAt?: string;  // from wal_events if tracked
}
```

POST /api/worktrees
- Body: { workspaceId, branchName, baseBranch?: string, worktreePath?: string }
- branchName: kebab-case, e.g. 'feat/user-auth-oauth'
- worktreePath: defaults to {workspacePath}/../{repoName}-{branchSlug}
- Runs: `git worktree add -b {branchName} {worktreePath} {baseBranch||HEAD}`
- Emits WAL event: event_type='worktree_created', payload: JSON.stringify({ branchName, worktreePath })
- Broadcasts to SSE: broadcast('all', 'worktree_created', { branchName })
- Returns { ok: true, worktreePath, branchName }
- Error handling: if branch already exists, return 409 with message

DELETE /api/worktrees/:encodedPath?workspaceId=
- encodedPath: URL-encoded absolute path of the worktree to remove
- Runs: `git worktree remove {decodedPath}` (fails if dirty, returns 409 with message)
- --force flag: if query param force=true, runs `git worktree remove --force {decodedPath}`
- Emits WAL event: event_type='worktree_removed'
- Returns { ok: true }

GET /api/worktrees/status?workspaceId=&path=
- Returns git status for a specific worktree: { branch, ahead, behind, isClean, modifiedFiles: string[] }
- Uses simple-git status() on the worktree path

File: ~/rorcha/backend/src/routes/__tests__/worktrees.test.ts
- Mock simple-git to avoid actual git operations in tests
- Test: GET /api/worktrees parses porcelain output correctly
- Test: POST /api/worktrees calls git.worktree.add with correct args
- Test: DELETE /api/worktrees/:path calls git.worktree.remove
- Test: 409 returned when branch already exists

The capstone story: Rorcha manages its own development. Register ~/rorcha/ as a workspace in its own SQLite DB, populate the Direction Stack with Rorcha's own architecture context, and generate a smoke-test spec.

This story is partly automated (API calls) and partly verified manually.

Automated setup script: ~/rorcha/scripts/self-register.ts
```typescript
// Run once: npx tsx scripts/self-register.ts
import { apiFetch } from './src/lib/apiFetch.ts'; // node-compatible version

const RORCHA_PATH = process.env.HOME + '/rorcha';

// 1. Create workspace
const ws = await apiFetch<{id:string}>('/api/workspaces', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify({ label: 'Rorcha', path: RORCHA_PATH, domains: ['typescript','vite','express','sqlite'] })
});

// 2. Seed L1 (global brain) from rorcha's own CLAUDE.md or README
const readmeContent = fs.readFileSync(RORCHA_PATH + '/README.md', 'utf8');
await apiFetch('/api/direction-stack', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify({ workspaceId: ws.id, layer: 1, contentYaml: `global:\n  readme: |\n${readmeContent.split('\n').map(l => '    ' + l).join('\n')}` })
});

// 3. Seed L3 (project brief) — Rorcha's own architecture
const l3Content = `project:\n  name: Rorcha\n  type: standalone-tool\n  stack: [vite, react, express, sqlite, better-sqlite3, simple-git]\n  purpose: AI spec orchestration dashboard\n  target_users: developers using Claude Code\n`;
await apiFetch('/api/direction-stack', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify({ workspaceId: ws.id, layer: 3, contentYaml: l3Content })
});

console.log('Self-registered! Workspace ID:', ws.id);
```

Add to backend/package.json scripts:
```json
"self-register": "npx tsx scripts/self-register.ts"
```

Smoke test (Ralph documents results):
1. cd ~/rorcha && npm run self-register
2. Open Rorcha UI → workspace selector shows 'Rorcha' workspace
3. Direction Stack Composer → L1 tab shows README content
4. Spec Forge → select Rorcha workspace → fill in 'Add workspace alias search to Archive panel'
5. Click Generate → spec appears with 4–6 stories
6. Navigate to Review Queue → spec appears pending
7. Approve → spec moves to approved
8. Archive panel → search 'archive' → spec appears in results

This confirms the full loop: Spec Forge → Review Queue → Archive works end-to-end on Rorcha's own codebase.
